- op: discuss  # 7c4f
  at: '2026-03-05T23:46:01Z'  # 7c4f
  by: agent  # 7c4f
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 7c4f
  clock: 3  # 7c4f
  nonce: 7c4f  # 7c4f
  message: "The stale-PR audit would work like this: during stop reflection, the hook queries Forgejo for open PRs on the repo (via scripts/ci-debug or a lightweight API call). Any PR older than 24h or with failed CI gets flagged in the guidance file as 'stale PR #NNN: created X hours ago, CI status: Y'. The agent would then either merge (if CI green), fix (if CI failed), or close (if superseded). This catches PRs that got orphaned due to context compaction or remote timeouts. Implementation: add a stale_pr_audit() function to stop_logic.sh that calls the Forgejo API (already allowlisted) and appends results to the guidance file."  # 7c4f
- op: discuss  # 3791
  at: '2026-03-06T06:18:16Z'  # 3791
  by: human  # 3791
  actor: jgstern  # 3791
  clock: 4  # 3791
  nonce: '3791'  # 3791
  message: "I would say this should apply for any PR created more than 6 hours ago, and leave CI status out of the filter, for simplicity. the way we determine whether CI has passed, failed, is pending, or has gone off the reservation and invented a new genre of young adult Magic Schoolbus fan fiction, is delicate and nuanced so be careful. rebasing probably will be needed here because the repo is set up to not allow merges of PRs that are out-of-date."  # 3791
- op: discuss  # e6f9
  at: '2026-03-06T07:36:18Z'  # e6f9
  by: agent  # e6f9
  actor: jgstern_agent  # e6f9
  clock: 5  # e6f9
  nonce: e6f9  # e6f9
  message: "Understood. Filter: any PR older than 6 hours, regardless of CI status. Rebase will be needed since repo requires up-to-date PRs. Will be careful with CI status detection — keep it simple."  # e6f9
- op: discuss  # b259
  at: '2026-03-06T15:14:18Z'  # b259
  by: human  # b259
  actor: jgstern  # b259
  clock: 6  # b259
  nonce: b259  # b259
  message: "keep the initial pass of deciding which PRs might be stale simple. but you can add as many nuances as you like to your decisionmaking in a second pass performed on the results of the first. I'm not saying \"don't be sophisticated\". Rather I'm saying \"be aware of the risks that come with being too clever, so manage your cleverness with structural safeguards and an appreciation of the stakes (fucking up CI fucks up everything and moreover the feedback loops are drawn out because it is CI)\". I approve you working on this item."  # b259
